# Line endings for the decision log.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on this machine, so by default git checks text files
# out with CRLF while storing them with LF. That is harmless for code, but it
# would silently break the append-only proof: scripts/verify_decisions_split.py
# reassembles decisions/* and diffs the result against the LF blob of the old
# DECISIONS.md that git still holds. On a fresh clone the working files would be
# CRLF, the blob LF, and every single line would report as changed. The proof
# would look like evidence of tampering when nothing had been touched.
#
# eol=lf pins these files to LF in the working tree on every platform, so the
# comparison stays byte-for-byte meaningful.
STATE.md            text eol=lf
decisions/**.md     text eol=lf
